<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Buyeo</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 494 CE (approx.) · capital Buyeo (near modern Jilin)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the Manchurian plains that Goguryeo and Baekje both claimed as their ancestral homeland, and a long-time ally of Han China.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Buyeo appears in Chinese sources in the 2nd century BCE as a farming and herding kingdom on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhua-river">Songhua</a> river plain around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin-city">Jilin</a>. Its founding legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dongmyeong-of-buyeo">Dongmyeong</a>, born of sunlight and escaping across a river on a bridge of fish and turtles, was later borrowed for the founder of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king shared power with four great officials named after animals: horse, ox, pig and dog, each governing a region. Kings could be blamed and deposed for bad harvests. Laws were strict, the yeonggo festival in the twelfth month honoured heaven, and nobles were buried with human victims. Buyeo usually sided with Chinese dynasties against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> raids under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> in 285 and 346 devastated the kingdom, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uiryeo-of-buyeo">Uiryeo</a> took his own life in the first. Weakened and pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a>, the last king surrendered to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> in 494. Baekje's later name <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/baekje">Nambuyeo</a> recalled the link.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/buyeo.jpg" alt="Buyeo" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3rd_century_Buyeo%E2%80%93Eastern_Buyeo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 494 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Buyeo (near modern Jilin)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Uiryeo of Buyeo, Wigutai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous sky worship</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Buyeo language</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Puyo, Fuyu, Northern Buyeo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=147&amp;focus=buyeo" aria-label="Buyeo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7672 606 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Buyeo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7992 862L7769 1191M7992 862L8115 1035"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7992 862h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7992 862h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=147&amp;focus=buyeo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

